  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on self-reported measures of physical functioning rather than objective clinical assessments.",
      "what": "Using survey data from 3,348 participants aged 49–76 in Brisbane, Australia, this study analyzed how transport modes and neighborhood disadvantage relate to self-reported physical functioning. The analysis revealed that women who use public transport have higher physical functioning than those relying on private transport, with physical functioning scores increasing by 10.36 points and 1.30 points for every 1 km closer they live to bus stops and train stations, respectively. No statistically significant associations between transport mode, neighborhood disadvantage, and physical functioning were found for men.",
      "why": "The study demonstrates that public transport accessibility acts as a critical moderator of physical health for mid-to-older women, particularly those residing in disadvantaged neighborhoods, establishing a gender-specific link between transit infrastructure and healthy aging."
    }
  },
